      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A care home logs falls, skin tears, medication errors and near misses all the time. Most stay in the home. A few must be reported by a deadline. The risk is missing one, or reporting it late.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Log every accident and incident, then review them for patterns.</li><li>Some work-related events must go to the HSE under RIDDOR 2013, by a deadline.</li><li>RIDDOR covers work-related harm. Not every resident fall is reportable.</li><li>Some events also go to the regulator: CQC in England, the Care Inspectorate in Scotland.</li><li>Quickler records the facts. It does not decide what is reportable. You or the registered person decide.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "RIDDOR",
      "heading": "When is it reportable to the HSE?",
      "html": "<p>RIDDOR 2013 covers work-related deaths, specified injuries, some injuries to non-workers, some diseases and dangerous occurrences. Each has its own deadline.</p><p>The key word is work-related. A fall from a resident's own frailty is not automatically reportable. A fall caused by how the work was done may be. Check the current HSE guidance case by case. This is general information, not legal advice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
